const MAGIC_COOKIE: u32 = 0x2112A442; // fixed constant that marks every STUN message

// A STUN Binding Request is just a 20-byte header: type, length, cookie, txid.
fn buildBindingRequest(txid: [12]u8) [20]u8 {
    var msg: [20]u8 = undefined;
    std.mem.writeInt(u16, msg[0..2], 0x0001, .big); // message type: Binding Request
    std.mem.writeInt(u16, msg[2..4], 0x0000, .big); // attribute length: none
    std.mem.writeInt(u32, msg[4..8], MAGIC_COOKIE, .big);
    @memcpy(msg[8..20], &txid); // 12-byte transaction id, echoed back in the reply
    return msg;
}
```

You send those 20 bytes over UDP to a public STUN server (Google runs one at `stun.l.google.com:19302`, and there are dozens of others), and it replies with a **Binding Success Response** containing your public mapping. The big-endian `writeInt` discipline is the same one we've named explicitly in every protocol of this arc (protobuf, DNS, HTTP/2, SOCKS5) -- on the wire, byte order is part of the contract, so we never leave it to chance and cross-compilation (episode 35) never reinterprets it behind our back.

The transaction id matters more than it looks. UDP gives you no connection, so the only way to match a reply to the request that caused it is that 12-byte id echoed verbatim. It's the same idea as the DNS query id from episode 82 -- correlation without a connection.

## Reading the XOR-MAPPED-ADDRESS

The response carries your mapping in an attribute called **XOR-MAPPED-ADDRESS** (type `0x0020`). Why XOR? Because some broken old NATs would inspect packets and \

 rewrite any bytes that looked like an IP address, corrupting the very answer STUN was trying to deliver. So STUN obfuscates the address by XOR-ing it with the magic cookie -- the NAT no longer recognises it as an address, but the client can trivially un-scramble it:

```zig
const Endpoint = struct { addr: [4]u8, port: u16 };

fn parseBindingResponse(msg: []const u8) !Endpoint {
    if (msg.len < 20) return error.ShortMessage;
    if (std.mem.readInt(u16, msg[0..2], .big) != 0x0101) return error.NotBindingSuccess;
    if (std.mem.readInt(u32, msg[4..8], .big) != MAGIC_COOKIE) return error.BadCookie;

    const attr_len = std.mem.readInt(u16, msg[2..4], .big);
    const end = @min(msg.len, 20 + @as(usize, attr_len));
    var off: usize = 20;
    while (off + 4 <= end) {
        const atyp = std.mem.readInt(u16, msg[off..][0..2], .big);
        const alen = std.mem.readInt(u16, msg[off + 2 ..][0..2], .big);
        if (off + 4 + alen > end) return error.TruncatedAttr;
        if (atyp == 0x0020) return parseXorMapped(msg[off + 4 ..][0..alen]);
        off += 4 + std.mem.alignForward(usize, alen, 4); // attributes are padded to 4 bytes
    }
    return error.NoMappedAddress;
}

fn parseXorMapped(val: []const u8) !Endpoint {
    if (val.len < 8) return error.ShortAttr;
    if (val[1] != 0x01) return error.NotIpv4; // family byte: 0x01 = IPv4
    // Port is XORed with the TOP 16 bits of the cookie; address with the whole cookie.
    const port = std.mem.readInt(u16, val[2..4], .big) ^ @as(u16, @truncate(MAGIC_COOKIE >> 16));
    const addr = std.mem.readInt(u32, val[4..8], .big) ^ MAGIC_COOKIE;
    var out: [4]u8 = undefined;
    std.mem.writeInt(u32, &out, addr, .big);
    return .{ .addr = out, .port = port };
}
```

The attribute walk is the kind of loop a fuzzer loves to break, so notice the two guards: `off + 4 + alen > end` catches a length that claims more bytes than the message holds, and the `@min` clamp keeps a lying `attr_len` field from sending us reading past the buffer. This is episode 4's philosophy made physical -- a hostile STUN server (or a corrupted packet) gets a named error, never a wild read.

Because this is pure byte parsing with no socket involved, it tests beautifully against a canned response. Let me encode a known mapping -- `203.0.113.5:50000` -- pre-XORed, and assert we recover it:

```zig
test \

 {
    // type=0x0101, attr-len=12, cookie, 12-byte txid, then one XOR-MAPPED-ADDRESS attr.
    // The 8 attr-value bytes encode 203.0.113.5:50000 already XORed with the cookie.
    const wire = [_]u8{ 0x01, 0x01, 0x00, 0x0C } ++ // header: success response, 12 attr bytes
        [_]u8{ 0x21, 0x12, 0xA4, 0x42 } ++ // magic cookie
        [_]u8{0xAA} ** 12 ++ // transaction id (arbitrary here)
        [_]u8{ 0x00, 0x20, 0x00, 0x08 } ++ // attr type 0x0020, length 8
        [_]u8{ 0x00, 0x01, 0xE2, 0x42, 0xEA, 0x12, 0xD5, 0x47 }; // family, x-port, x-addr

    const ep = try parseBindingResponse(&wire);
    try std.testing.expectEqual([4]u8{ 203, 0, 113, 5 }, ep.addr);
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(u16, 50000), ep.port);
}
```

If you want to convince yourself those scrambled bytes are right: `50000` is `0xC350`, and `0xC350 ^ 0x2112` (the top half of the cookie) is `0xE242`. The address XOR works the same way over all four bytes. Un-scrambling is its own inverse, which is the whole charm of XOR.

## NAT types, and the one that ruins everything

Here's where honesty matters, because hole punching is _not_ guaranteed to work. NAT behaviour falls into a few buckets, and they decide your fate:

```zig
const NatType = enum {
    open,       // no NAT, or full-cone: the mapping accepts packets from anyone
    restricted, // address/port-restricted cone: peer reachable only after we send first
    symmetric,  // a NEW public port per destination -> the punch usually fails
};
```

The first two are friendly. A **restricted-cone** NAT (by far the most common home router) keeps one public port for your socket and lets anyone reply to it _once you've sent to them first_ -- which is exactly the precondition hole punching arranges. The killer is **symmetric** NAT: it assigns a _different_ public port for every distinct destination. So the mapping STUN discovered (when you talked to the STUN server) is useless for talking to your peer, because the NAT will mint a fresh, unpredictable port the moment you aim at a new address. When either side is symmetric, the port you tell your peer to aim at is already wrong.

Detecting your type means sending binding requests to two different STUN servers and comparing the mappings: same public port for both means cone (good), different ports means symmetric (bad). When the punch can't work, production systems fall back to a **relay** -- which is exactly the proxy from episode 93, wearing the name TURN. _Having said that_, real-world numbers are encouraging: somewhere around 80-90% of peer pairs can punch successfully, and only the unlucky symmetric-to-symmetric combinations are forced onto a relay. The proxy never dies; it just becomes plan B.

## The rendezvous: introducing two strangers

The peers still can't talk to each other to coordinate the punch -- that's the whole problem. So we need one publicly reachable machine they _can_ both reach: a **rendezvous** (or signaling) server. Its job is almost insultingly small. Each peer runs STUN, learns its own public endpoint, and uploads those six bytes to the rendezvous. The server pairs them up and hands each peer the other's endpoint. Then it gets out of the way -- no application data ever flows through it:

```zig
// The endpoints get swapped through the rendezvous as six flat bytes: 4 addr + 2 port.
fn encodeEndpoint(ep: Endpoint) [6]u8 {
    var out: [6]u8 = undefined;
    @memcpy(out[0..4], &ep.addr);
    std.mem.writeInt(u16, out[4..6], ep.port, .big);
    return out;
}

fn decodeEndpoint(b: [6]u8) std.net.Address {
    return std.net.Address.initIp4(b[0..4].*, std.mem.readInt(u16, b[4..6], .big));
}

test \

 {
    const ep = Endpoint{ .addr = .{ 203, 0, 113, 5 }, .port = 50000 };
    const back = decodeEndpoint(encodeEndpoint(ep));
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(u16, 50000), back.getPort());
}
```

Notice this is the same six-byte endpoint encoding STUN handed us, and the same one we'll feed straight into the punch. One representation, three uses (discover, exchange, punch) -- the parse-once-pass-around discipline we've kept since the file-sync project. The rendezvous itself is just a tiny TCP server (the accept loop from episode 51) holding a `HashMap` of waiting peers (episode 22); it touches none of the bytes the peers will eventually exchange.

## The punch

Now the moment of truth. Both peers, having swapped public endpoints, fire UDP packets at each other from the _same socket they used for STUN_. That detail is non-negotiable: the mapping STUN discovered belongs to that specific local port, so the punch must reuse it (set `SO_REUSEADDR` and bind the same local port for both). Each side sends and listens in a tight loop -- the outbound packets open the local NAT's door, and within a few round trips an inbound packet slips through:

```zig
// Both peers call this with the other's public endpoint. The socket is non-blocking
// and bound to the SAME local port used for the STUN request earlier.
fn punch(sock: std.posix.socket_t, peer: std.net.Address) !void {
    const hello = \

; // any small payload; we only care that a packet arrives
    var attempt: u32 = 0;
    while (attempt < 50) : (attempt += 1) {
        // Always send: each outbound packet (re)opens our own NAT mapping toward the peer.
        _ = std.posix.sendto(sock, hello, 0, &peer.any, peer.getOsSockLen()) catch {};

        var buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        var from: std.posix.sockaddr = undefined;
        var len: std.posix.socklen_t = @sizeOf(std.posix.sockaddr);
        const n = std.posix.recvfrom(sock, &buf, 0, &from, &len) catch |e| switch (e) {
            error.WouldBlock => { // nothing through yet -- wait a tick and fire again
                std.time.sleep(50 * std.time.ns_per_ms);
                continue;
            },
            else => return e,
        };
        if (n > 0) return; // a packet from the peer means BOTH holes are open
    }
    return error.PunchFailed; // give up; the caller falls back to a relay
}
```

The shape is symmetric on purpose -- there is no client and no server here, just two equal peers doing the identical thing. The first few packets each side sends will be dropped by the _other_ NAT (no mapping yet), but each dropped packet still created a mapping on the _sender's_ side. After a round or two, both mappings exist, and the next packets sail through in both directions. The `WouldBlock` handling is why the socket must be non-blocking: a blocking `recvfrom` would stall, so we couldn't keep sending while we wait, and the simultaneity that makes the whole trick work would be lost.

Once a packet arrives, you've got a direct peer-to-peer UDP path -- and from there it's exactly the territory of episode 81 (datagrams) plus whatever reliability layer you care to build on top. The hole, by the way, is not permanent: NATs expire idle mappings (often after 30 seconds for UDP), so a long-lived peer connection sends a tiny keepalive packet every 15-20 seconds to keep the door from quietly closing. Forget that and your connection mysteriously dies after half a minute of silence -- a bug that has eaten _quite some_ developer evenings.

## Performance and reliability considerations

The good news: once the hole is open, there is zero per-packet overhead. No proxy, no relay, no protocol framing -- the data plane is just `sendto`/`recvfrom` between two real endpoints, as fast as the network allows. All the cost is in setup: two STUN round trips, a rendezvous exchange, and the punch loop, all of which complete in well under a second on a healthy network.

The reliability story is more nuanced and worth being honest about. Hole punching has a _success rate_, not a guarantee. Symmetric NATs, carrier-grade NAT (where your ISP NATs you a second time and you never see a usable mapping at all), and corporate firewalls that block UDP outright will all defeat it. A production system therefore always pairs the punch with a relay fallback -- and accidentically shipping without that fallback is the classic mistake, because it works perfectly on your home network and then fails for the 15% of users behind hostile NATs. Measure your real population, _then_ tune how aggressively you retry before giving up (episode 34's profiling rule, applied to a network instead of a CPU).

## How this compares to C, Rust, and Go

In **C**, the STUN parsing and the punch loop look almost identical to ours -- it's all `sendto`/`recvfrom` and byte fiddling, which is C's home turf. What C lacks is the compiler holding you to bounds: that attribute-walk loop, where we guarded against a lying `alen`, is exactly where a real CVE has lived in more than one C STUN library. The footgun is right there.

